<& /page/page_title.mas, title => 'Instructions for using the <span class="secretary">SecreTary</span> signal peptide predictor' &>

<& /secretom/secretary/nav.mas, curr_page => 'instructions' &>

<div class="indented_content">

  <p>
   <& /util/sentence.mas, text => __x('{span}SecreTary{end_span} is for prediction of signal peptides from the amino acid sequence of a protein.'), span => '<span class="secretary">', end_span => '</span>' &> Signal peptides generally possess a common
   structural design, which is characterized by a short, positively
   charged n-region followed by a hydrophobic region (h-region) and a
   somewhat polar and uncharged cleavage site (c-region). It should be
   more emphasized that the h-region is known to be important for the
   translocation of the signal peptides to the ER. To improve the
   performance of the signal peptide prediction, we have developed new
   parameters such as the overall hydrophobicity and amino acid
   composition in the h-region as input. <& /util/sentence.mas, text => __x('{span}SecreTary{end_span} is mainly trained and tested on the plant secretome that has been identified through functional screens including a yeast secretion trap.'), span => '<span class="secretary">', end_span => '</span>' &>
  </p>

  <p>
   Questions or suggestions about <span class="secretary">SecreTary</span> should be addressed to
   Jocelyn K.C. Rose (<a href="mailto:jr286@cornell.edu">
   jr286@cornell.edu</a>).
  </p>

</div>

<&| /page/info_section.mas, title => $m->interp->apply_escapes( __('1. Input Sequences'), 'h' ) &>

  <p>
  <& /util/sentence.mas, text => __x('The amino acid sequences can be input by pasting one or more protein sequences, in {bold}FASTA format{end_bold} into the {span}SecreTary{end_span} input page.'), bold => '<b>', end_bold => '</b>', span => '<span class="secretary">', end_span => '</span>' &> <& /util/sentence.mas, text => __x('{span}SecreTary{end_span} analyses proteins at a rate of approximate 80/second.'), span => '<span class="secretary">', end_span => '</span>' &> At present there is a limit of 10000 sequences. All the
  input sequences must be in one-letter amino acid code, not case-sensitive.
  All letters of the alphabet are accepted, but <b>B J O U </b> and <b>Z</b>
  are replaced by <b>X</b> before analysis.
  </p>

  <h3><% __('An example:') |h %></h3>
  <& /sequence/with_markup.mas,
         seq => Bio::PrimarySeq->new(
                     -id => 'P05117_Tomato',
                     -seq => '',
                 ),
          subdiv => 0,
          width => 100,
   &>

</&>

<&| /page/info_section.mas, title => $m->interp->apply_escapes( __('2. Submission'), 'h' ) &>

  <& /util/sentence.mas, text => __x('Paste the sequence(s) into the window, or click browse to select a fasta file on your disk for analysis. Then, click on the "Submit" button. Expect to wait about 10-15 seconds to see the results of analyzing 1000 sequences.') &>

</&>

<style type="text/css">
.yellow { background: yellow }
.blue { background: #aaf }

</style>
<&| /page/info_section.mas, title => $m->interp->apply_escapes( __('3. Output'), 'h' ) &>

  <p>
  <& /util/sentence.mas, text => __x('For each sequence {span}SecreTary{end_span} gives a score; sequences with scores greater than or equal to 0.75 are predicted to have a signal peptide.'), span => '<span class="secretary">', end_span => '</span>' &> 
  For each predicted signal peptide the 
  predicted length is shown, and the location of the hydrophobic
  region (in 
  <FONT style=BACKGROUND-COLOR: #AAAAFF">blue</FONT>
  ) within the predicted signal peptide (in 
  <FONT style=BACKGROUND-COLOR: #FFDD66">yellow</FONT>
  ). On the input page you can choose
  to sort the output by score, with the higher scores (strongest
  candidates) at the top, and you can suppress output for sequences
  with no predicted signal peptide.
  </p>

  <h3><% __('An example:') |h %></h3>
  <pre>

